WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.820
Good morning, John. I was just taking my dog for a walk.

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:05.400
And I thought to myself: Hank, you know what you should do today?

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:08.080
You should BLOW JOHN GREEN'S MIND!

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:11.700
So that's what I've decided to do, John. Get ready to have your mind blown.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:15.580
So let's start right here on Earth. Earth is big. It's massive. It's freakin' huge.

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:19.220
It's so big that we didn't know how big it was until, like, a few hundred years ago!

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:24.080
That's not actually true. People had figured out how big the Earth was a long, long time ago using math and being really smart.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:27.200
But we certainly hadn't mapped the whole thing! I mean, five hundred years ago,

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:30.880
Columbus was sailing around thinking he was gonna run into India because he didn't know

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:32.820
that North America was there!

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:36.600
But John, as massive as the Earth is, the sun is much bigger.

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:39.840
As They Might Be Giants have, I'm sure, informed you, if the sun were hollow,

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:41.760
a million earths would fit inside!

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:44.680
So John - I know I didn't just blow your mind - there's more coming.

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:50.480
Our sun is very big. In our galaxy, there are AT LEAST 200 BILLION of them!

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:56.220
It could be as many as 400 billion. Turns out, NOT EASY to count all the stars in the galaxy.

00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:00.860
I know, I know I have not blown your mind yet. That is not even the mind-blowing stuff.

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:06.700
The Hubble Space Telescope is the most powerful telescope ever created by human kind.

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:09.760
I say human kind, because, ah, by the end of this video,

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:14.580
you will probably think there are other large-telescope-wielding-species in the universe.

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:18.640
John, right now, I am going to draw a dot on my hand. DOT.

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:22.300
Okay, you see that dot? Now I'm gonna move that dot out here.

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:26.960
This is the size of a piece of our universe that the Hubble Space Telescope was once pointed at.

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:33.380
A scientist at NASA pointed that space telescope at that tiny, tiny bit of our sky.

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:36.240
This tiny dot of sky was rare.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:41.120
Because there was nothing there. There were no stars in our own galaxy, there were no galaxies that we could see.

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:49.340
There were no nebulae or any smudge of anything. It was just a pure, black spot of sky.

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:55.340
Now some people were like, WTF NASA? Why are you taking this extremely expensive piece of machinery

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:57.160
and taking a picture of nothing with it?

00:01:57.160 --> 00:02:02.140
They did it. They pointed it at that little piece of sky and they opened the shutter and they left it open

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:07.900
for days. They'd done it once before, but now they've done it again in December 2009.

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:10.600
It's called the Hubble Ultra-Deep Field.

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:16.260
When they downloaded the picture of that speck of nothing, that grain of inky black

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:20.160
contained over THREE THOUSAND GALAXIES!

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:28.020
Galaxies, just like the one that our massive planet takes up a tiny fraction of a corner of a crevice.

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:36.840
Every dot - every smudge, every speck in this image - is a galaxy with BILLIONS of stars in it.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:39.900
That is a big universe, and let me blow your mind further

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:42.580
by telling you that I think we have an important place in it.

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:48.440
We don't know what's going on. It took humanity 99.9% of its existence

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:53.460
to even understand that we live on a ball of rock, circling a globe of plasma.

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:59.860
When you watch that video, you understand the scope of the universe. That understanding, the act of understanding,

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:07.720
I think, is more important, in itself, than the existence of all star stuff in the universe.

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:13.860
People have a lot of different reactions to the knowledge that we are on a tiny pale blue dot.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:19.640
But that's mine, and I want to know what yours is. So leave them down in the comments (in my pants).

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:21.534
John, I'll see you on Wednesday.

